          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is coronary artery disease and how does it develop over time?",
          "answer": "Coronary artery disease is the build-up of atherosclerotic plaque in the arteries that supply the heart muscle. The source is the \"Coronary artery disease\" educational summary. It says plaque develops over decades, starting with injury to the artery lining and a buildup of cholesterol-rich material beneath it.\n\nThe same summary describes two ways the disease causes problems:\n\n- **Gradual narrowing** limits blood flow when the heart needs more of it. This produces predictable chest discomfort during exertion, called stable angina.\n- **Plaque rupture** can trigger a clot that suddenly blocks the artery. This is an acute coronary syndrome. It can happen at a plaque that never narrowed the artery enough to cause symptoms, so a normal stress test in the past does not rule out a heart attack now.\n\nThe \"Heart failure\" summary notes that coronary artery disease and previous heart attacks are the most common causes of heart failure. The \"Hypertension\" summary says long-term high blood pressure damages arteries over years and can lead to heart attack.\n\nThe retrieved passages did not cover the specific risk factors for coronary artery disease, its full list of symptoms, how it is diagnosed, or how it is treated. If you have questions about your own heart health, a clinician is the right person to ask.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
